      "writtenOn": "2022-12-27T08:31:52Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Frankly this scheme looks very baroque. You need to have temporary storage, and the storage is shared through members with not even as much as comments for documentation.\n\nWhy not just use recursion here ? Something like :\n\n    private void performTransitions() {\n      if (null \u003d\u003d mDestState) return;\n      final StateInfo commonAncestor \u003d getLastActiveAncestor(mStateInfo.get(mDestState));\n      executeExitMethods(commonAncestor, mCurrentState);\n      executeEnterMethods(commonAncestor, mDestState);\n      mCurrentState \u003d mDestState;\n      mDestState \u003d null;\n    }\n\n    private StateInfo getLastActiveAncestor(@NonNull final StateInfo start) {\n      return (null \u003d\u003d start || start.mActive) ? start : getLastActiveAncestor(start.parent);\n    }\n\n    private void executeExitMethods(@NonNull StateInfo dest, @NonNull StateInfo start) {\n      if (dest \u003d\u003d start) return;\n      start.state.exit();\n      executeExitMethods(dest, start.parent);\n    }\n\n    private void executeEnterMethods(@NonNull StateInfo start, @NonNull StateInfo dest) {\n      if (dest \u003d\u003d start) return;\n      executeEnterMethods(start, dest.parent);\n      dest.enter();\n    }\n\nIsn\u0027t that much simpler ?\nYou could even imagine one less iteration by descending the stack first and calling the exits inline, but I think that would be harder to read.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 165,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 168,
        "endChar": 36
      },
      "revId": "8070911e15d2d4136a3a8cda5cfbac6b3db73e4d",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
